findings.body:
The new `request()` still declares `verify=None` and `cert=None` (lines 148-149) but never assigns them to `req`, never stores them on the session, and never passes them into `self.send(prep)`. `send()` (lines 364-367) constructs a bare `HTTPAdapter()` with no arguments, so nothing the caller said about TLS reaches the transport.

Consequences depend on the adapter's default, none of which is good:
- a caller passing `verify='/path/to/corporate-ca.pem'` gets validated against some other (or no) trust store, so a certificate the caller would have rejected is accepted;
- a caller passing `cert=(client.pem, key.pem)` loses mutual-TLS client auth;
- if `HTTPAdapter()` defaults to not verifying, every HTTPS request on this path is unauthenticated.

Adversary: anyone in a network position between the client and the origin (hostile Wi-Fi, compromised upstream proxy, DNS/BGP hijack). They control the TLS peer certificate presented; they get plaintext request bodies, `Authorization` headers and cookies set on line 154/158/159. Fix by threading `verify`/`cert` (and `timeout`) through `prepare()`/`send()` to the adapter before this path replaces `old_request`.
